* Their-side inter-QM objects (#147): {{ qmgr_name }} <-> {{ our_qm }} across net-ext.
* {{ our_qm }}'s SENDER connects in to RECEIVER {{ our_qm }}.{{ qmgr_name }}; replies
* leave via SENDER {{ qmgr_name }}.{{ our_qm }} over xmitq {{ our_qm }}, whose CONNAME
* lists BOTH our site VIPs so it follows our QM across HA (same site) and DR (other
* site). Retry timers tuned for lab snappiness (spec §5.2 / §6).
DEFINE QLOCAL(SVC.REQUEST) DEFPSIST(YES) REPLACE
* The co-located service responder connects client-mode via this SVRCONN (#180):
* pip's pymqi is client-only, so a true bindings connect fails 2058. MCAUSER(mqm)
* gives it admin authority; CHLAUTH is already disabled on this QM.
DEFINE CHANNEL(SVC.SVRCONN) CHLTYPE(SVRCONN) TRPTYPE(TCP) MCAUSER('mqm') SSLCIPH({{ tls_cipher }}) SSLCAUTH(REQUIRED) SSLPEER('{{ tls_peer_dtcc }}') REPLACE
* Monitoring SVRCONN for our client-org ops exporter (#250/#182). QMDTCC trusts the
* client-org CA (QM-link), so it validates the exporter's O=client-org cert; SSLPEER
* pins it. Distinct from SVC.SVRCONN (the O=dtcc-org responder) so each client's peer
* identity stays exact.
DEFINE CHANNEL(MON.SVRCONN) CHLTYPE(SVRCONN) TRPTYPE(TCP) MCAUSER('mqm') SSLCIPH({{ tls_cipher }}) SSLCAUTH(REQUIRED) SSLPEER('{{ tls_peer_client }}') REPLACE
DEFINE CHANNEL({{ our_qm }}.{{ qmgr_name }}) CHLTYPE(RCVR) TRPTYPE(TCP) SSLCIPH({{ tls_cipher }}) SSLCAUTH(REQUIRED) SSLPEER('{{ tls_peer_inhouse }}') REPLACE
DEFINE QLOCAL({{ our_qm }}) USAGE(XMITQ) TRIGGER TRIGTYPE(FIRST) INITQ(SYSTEM.CHANNEL.INITQ) TRIGDATA({{ qmgr_name }}.{{ our_qm }}) REPLACE
DEFINE CHANNEL({{ qmgr_name }}.{{ our_qm }}) CHLTYPE(SDR) TRPTYPE(TCP) CONNAME('{{ our_conn }}') XMITQ({{ our_qm }}) SSLCIPH({{ tls_cipher }}) SSLPEER('{{ tls_peer_inhouse }}') SHORTRTY(10) SHORTTMR(5) LONGRTY(999999999) LONGTMR(20) REPLACE
